      "snapshot": "Short Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n226 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nValid 00Z Mon Nov 23 2026 - 00Z Wed Nov 25 2026\n\n...Coastal low to bring heavy rain and gusty winds to New England\ntonight into Monday, with minor coastal flooding possible along\nparts of the East coast...\n\n...Much colder air settles over the central and eastern U.S. to\nstart Thanksgiving week, with lake-effect snow and the season's\nfirst freeze for parts of Texas...\n\n...Unsettled weather continues in the Pacific Northwest and\nnorthern California, with rain, mountain snow, gusty winds, and a\ngrowing coastal flooding threat..\n\n\nAn area of low pressure tracking up the East Coast will pass east\nof the New Jersey shore tonight and move across the Gulf of Maine\non Monday. Moderate to locally heavy rain will spread across New\nEngland tonight and continue into Monday morning, with the\nheaviest totals expected along the coastal plain. Some nuisance\nstreet flooding is possible during the Monday morning commute. The\nrain will be welcome in northern New England and Maine, where\nongoing drought has left water supplies low. Large waves and high\nastronomical tides ahead of Tuesday's full moon may cause minor\ncoastal flooding at times along the Mid-Atlatnic and Southeast\ncoasts. Beach erosion and ocean overwash are possible on the Outer\nBanks. Rain will taper off across the East by Monday afternoon as\ndrier air moves in.\n\nMeanwhile, a strong cold front will sweep through the Ohio and\nTennessee Valleys and the Southeast tonight and Monday. Scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected along the front. Behind it,\na much colder air mass will settle over the central and eastern\nU.S. into midweek, with highs mainly in the 30s and 40s across the\nMidwest. Cold northwest winds crossing the Great Lakes will produce\nlake-effect snow showers on Monday, with light accumulations in the\nfavored snowbelts of the Upper Peninsula of Michigan and northern\nWisconsin. Farther south, the cold air will bring the first freeze\nof the season to parts of west and north Texas, and hard freezes\nare possible across Oklahoma by Tuesday night. A fast-moving\ndisturbance may also bring light snow and a wintry mix to New\nMexico and the southern High Plains Monday night into Tuesday\nmorning, with slick spots possible.\n\nIn the west, a series of Pacific systems will keep the pattern\nactive across the Pacific northwest and northern California. A\nstronger system will arrive Tuesday with widespread rain, mountain\nsnow, gusty coastal winds, and high surf. Very high astronomical\ntides combined with these storms will increase the risk of coastal\nflooding along the Washington coast and around San Francisco Bay\nthis week, with major to extreme coastal flooding possible later in\nthe week. Snow will make travel difficult over Cascade passes at\ntimes.\n\nLooking ahead, rain will spread into the Gulf Coast and Southeast\nTuesday night ahead of a developing storm system. That system is\nexpected to bring heavy rain, strong winds, coastal flooding, and\nsome wintry weather to parts of the East around Thanksgiving.\nTravelers should closely monitor the forecast.\n\nEovino\n\n\nGraphics available at\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php</u></a>"
    },
