      "snapshot": "Extended Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n300 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nValid 12Z Wed Nov 25 2026 - 12Z Sun Nov 29 2026\n\n\n...Holiday storm to bring heavy rain, strong winds and high surf\nto the East Coast, with some wintry weather potential from the\nCentral Appalachians up through the interior Northeast...\n\n...Wet pattern continues through holiday weekend for West Coast...\n\n\n...Overview...\n\nA pair of mid-level disturbances phase and amplify over the\nEastern half of the country while moving into the Canadian\nMaritime later this week. These systems will be responsible for\nhazardous weather that occurs across the East Coast and interior\nareas through the Thanksgiving holiday. Diffluent to zonal flow\nover the Western U.S. should support multiple days of precipitation\nfor the Northwest Coast and some inland areas.\n\n\n...Guidance/Predictability Assessment...\n\nModels are fairly agreeable on day 3, with only minor timing\ndifferences between the deterministic GFS and Euro with respect to\nthe northern stream disturbance. The southern stream system is a\nnewer development on the latest model runs, but is still captured\nwell by the ensemble means and the EC/GFS. Pattern predictability\ndrops considerably by day 4, probably due to uncertainty on when\nexactly the two streams will phase. Models appear more agreeable\nwith a closed low over the Great Lakes by day 5, however, some\npositional discrepancies allow for more ensemble mean weighting in\nthe blend. The ensemble mean weights are gradually increased\nthrough day 7 due to increased pattern variability in the\ndeterministic guidance.\n\n\n...Weather/Hazards Highlights.\n\nA coastal low, supported by a potent southern stream disturbance\nwill begin spreading heavy rain and strong winds along the\nSoutheast and Mid-Atlantic Coasts on Wednesday. Heavy to excessive\nrainfall is expected to occur along the immediate coast from South\nCarolina up to the Virginia Tidewater. A slight risk (at least 15%\nchance) of excessive rainfall is in effect for portions of the\nCarolina Coast east of I-95. Strong winds, gusting to over 40 kts\nat times, could contribute to beach erosion, high surf and coastal\nflooding on Wednesday.\n\nThe system intensifies and lifts north on Thanksgiving day. Heavy\nrain and damaging winds spread into the Mid- Atlantic and\nNortheast (I-95 corridor included), where a marginal risk (at least\n5% chance) of excessive rainfall is in effect. The lack of\ninstability is a mitigating factor for a more elevated/widespread\nflash flood risk at this time. However, coastal/areal flooding and\nbeach erosion remain a concern. Some minor to moderately impactful\nsnowfall may develop over portions of the Central Appalachians,\nLower Great Lakes and interior Northeast/New England on\nThanksgiving and continue into Friday. Windy conditions with\nrain and snow showers are expected to linger across portions of\nNew England on Friday before things begin to improve next weekend.\n\nAn active pattern continues across the Northwest as a series of\nlow pressure systems lash the coastline this week. Marginal risks\n(at least 5% chance) of excessive rainfall are in effect from\nnorthern California to the Olympic Peninsula of Washington on\nWednesday and Thursday. Snow is expected for the highest elevations\nof the Cascades down to the northern California Sierra.\n\n\nKebede\n\n\nAdditional 3-7 Day Hazard information can be found on the WPC\nmedium range hazards outlook chart at:\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/threats/threats.php\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/threats/threats.php</u></a>\n\nWPC medium range 500mb heights, surface systems, weather grids,\nquantitative precipitation forecast, excessive rainfall outlook,\nwinter weather outlook probabilities, heat indices and Key Messages\nare at:\n\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst500_wbg.gif\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst500_wbg.gif</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst_wbg_conus.gif\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst_wbg_conus.gif</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/5km_grids/5km_gridsbody.html\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/5km_grids/5km_gridsbody.html</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/qpf/day4-7.shtml\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/qpf/day4-7.shtml</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ero\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ero</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/pwpf_d47/pwpf_medr.php?day=4\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/pwpf_d47/pwpf_medr.php?day=4</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/heat_index.shtml\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/heat_index.shtml</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ovw\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ovw</u></a>"
